# .claude/review-config — Sol reviewer lane bounded-diligence caps
# (v4.0.0 Phase V2). Same key=value format as .claude/rubric-config.
#
# THE ONE PLACE caps live. codex-review.sh reads these to bound the Sol
# (Codex) review turn: it never loops past a cap; a cap-hit sets the
# artifact's `stopped_by` field (cap:max_findings | cap:max_review_iterations
# | cap:timeout) and stops. Sol is ADVISORY and STRICTLY OPTIONAL — these
# caps only shape the optional Codex turn; the Claude review path and the
# qa-gate release credential are unaffected.
#
# Comments (lines starting with #) and blank lines are ignored. Whitespace
# around the = is tolerated. Unrecognised keys are ignored; a missing key
# or file resolves to the built-in default noted below (fail-open).
#
# Recognised keys:
#   risk_threshold_default   default blocking bar when a review request omits
#                            one. Ordered severity enum
#                            critical>high>medium>low>info. Default: high.
#   max_findings             hard cap on findings in one artifact. Excess is
#                            truncated (most-severe-first) and stopped_by is
#                            set to cap:max_findings. Default: 10.
#   max_review_iterations    hard cap on review iterations for a task; an
#                            iteration above this exits before any Codex call.
#                            Built-in default when unset: 3. Shipped: 12 —
#                            see the rationale below.
#   timeout_seconds          overall wall-clock budget for one Codex turn;
#                            exceeding it kills the server and writes no
#                            artifact (the caller degrades to the Claude
#                            path). Built-in default when unset: 300.
#                            Shipped: 2400 — see the rationale below.
#   malformed_retry          number of corrective codex-reply turns allowed
#                            when the model's first message fails artifact
#                            validation. Default: 1.
#   max_request_bytes        hard cap on the REQUEST FILE'S byte size (the
#                            file codex-review.sh receives via --request, NOT
#                            the wrapped envelope it stages for jq --rawfile).
#                            Checked BEFORE the Codex server is spawned;
#                            exceeding it exits 7 with no artifact and no
#                            spend. Built-in default when unset: 100000.
#                            Shipped: 100000 — see the rationale below.
#
# WHY THE SHIPPED VALUES ARE 12 AND 2400 (iu5o, operator decision 2026-08-10;
# grader conditions from fkm.1.11 rubric v2: rationale in-file, CHANGELOG
# entry, and an L2 leg asserting THESE shipped values — codex-review.sh spec
# section C8 brackets max_review_iterations at the rc=6 boundary and pins
# timeout_seconds, both against this file's bytes, so drift here fails the
# tier instead of riding invisibly the way the first raise did).
#
# The measurement that drove this cuts AGAINST the larger numbers, and it
# belongs here more than the numbers do. On the first correctly-invoked paid
# Sol relay (fkm.1.11 arc, 2026-08-09), Sol completed a real 1008-line scoped
# review payload in 66 SECONDS of the original 300 — the timeout was never
# the blocker. What actually blocked the turn was max_review_iterations=3
# returning rc=6 at ZERO seconds ("iteration 7 exceeds
# max_review_iterations=3"), because the iteration counter DOES NOT
# DISTINGUISH CODEX TURNS FROM CLAUDE ONES: a change set that has consumed
# several Claude review rounds becomes permanently ineligible for its first
# Codex turn — and the task that needed many rounds is exactly the task where
# a second model family is most valuable. Raising the cap to 12 treats that
# symptom so the lane is usable at all; the lane-blind counter is the defect,
# and it is owned by claude-workflow-plugin-nq5f (make the counter per-lane).
# When nq5f lands, this cap should be revisited DOWN.
#
# timeout_seconds=2400 is headroom, not measurement: the only payload that
# ever exhausted 300s was a 541KB unscoped request (2026-08-06) whose failure
# is undiagnosable in hindsight (the driver discarded error_key — fkm.1.14),
# while the measured real review used 66s. 2400 exists so a large legitimate
# turn cannot die at the budget while nq5f/fkm.1.14 make the curve
# measurable; it does not raise per-turn spend for well-behaved turns (Sol
# bills by work, and a fast turn still returns in seconds) — what it does
# raise is how long a WEDGED turn can hold the machine before the caller
# degrades to the Claude path: 40 minutes instead of 5. That trade is
# accepted, stated, and bounded by exactly this line.
#
# WHY max_request_bytes=100000 (nq5f, operator-recorded measurement,
# 2026-08-13; see the comment thread on claude-workflow-plugin-nq5f — these
# are the operator's own reported round-trip figures, not a command this file
# re-ran). Two points are MEASURED; 100000 is CHOSEN from them, not one of
# them: a ~88KB request completed in 326s, 493s, 496s, 613s, 635s across five
# separate rounds; a ~144KB request EXHAUSTED the entire 2400s
# timeout_seconds budget above, with NO artifact and (before the fkm.1.14
# fold-in below) no way to tell "still generating" from "server exited". The
# two points are NOT LINEAR — a 1.6x size increase did not cost 1.6x the
# time, it cost the whole budget — and two points cannot be interpolated or
# extrapolated into a curve regardless: deriving a relationship needs a third
# measurement at minimum, which does not exist yet. 100000 sits BETWEEN the
# two, rounded for legibility (a round number an operator reads correctly
# under time pressure) and closer to the confirmed-good side than the
# confirmed-bad one — a JUDGMENT CALL, not a derivation, and EXPECTED TO MOVE
# once a third measurement exists to reason from. It errs LOW on purpose: a
# refusal at this bound is a CHEAP wrong answer (rescope the request smaller,
# re-run, seconds lost), while a timeout at 2400s is an EXPENSIVE one (40
# minutes of nothing) — that cost asymmetry, not the measurement, is why the
# bound sits toward the cheap side of the uncertainty rather than centred in
# it. D2's packet (review artifact + grilling record + impact report +
# lessons) is expected to exceed 144KB on its own, which is exactly why this
# refusal has to exist BEFORE D2 lands, not after the first D2 review turn
# silently burns 40 minutes the same way. Revisit UP only against a new
# measurement, never against a guess.

risk_threshold_default=high
max_findings=10
max_review_iterations=12
timeout_seconds=2400
malformed_retry=1
max_request_bytes=100000
